WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:03.399
Two rivers run through the middle of Chennai, the Cooum and the

00:03.399 --> 00:06.600
Adyar. For most of the year they are slow and shallow.

00:06.600 --> 00:09.927
For a few days in a big monsoon, they become some of the most

00:09.927 --> 00:11.473
powerful things in the city.

00:11.473 --> 00:14.693
To understand why, you need to know how a river works:

00:14.693 --> 00:18.225
where its water comes from, where it speeds up and slows down,

00:18.225 --> 00:20.041
and what it builds along the way.

00:20.041 --> 00:22.321
Every river has a drainage basin.

00:22.321 --> 00:24.953
This is all the land that drains into that river.

00:24.953 --> 00:28.829
Rain that falls anywhere inside the basin runs downhill into small

00:28.829 --> 00:30.365
streams called tributaries.

00:30.365 --> 00:33.878
The tributaries join the main channel, and the main channel carries

00:33.878 --> 00:35.434
the water to the river's mouth.

00:35.434 --> 00:37.894
The edge of the basin is called the divide.

00:37.894 --> 00:39.747
It is the high ground around the outside.

00:39.747 --> 00:43.234
Rain that falls on the far side of the divide goes to a different

00:43.234 --> 00:46.944
river. This matters, because a river responds to rain across its

00:46.944 --> 00:50.692
whole basin. Heavy rain far upstream can raise the river in the

00:50.692 --> 00:53.081
city hours later, even under a clear sky.

00:53.081 --> 00:55.956
Now follow a river from the hills to the sea.

00:55.956 --> 00:59.834
In the hills the river is steep and fast, and it mostly erodes.

00:59.834 --> 01:03.732
On flatter land, the channel, which is the path the water normally

01:03.732 --> 01:05.967
flows in, winds across a floodplain.

01:05.967 --> 01:09.511
The floodplain is the flat land on either side that the river

01:09.511 --> 01:10.894
covers during a flood.

01:10.894 --> 01:13.722
Near the coast the river reaches its estuary.

01:13.722 --> 01:17.417
Here fresh river water mixes with salt water, and the tide moves

01:17.417 --> 01:20.877
in and out. If the river brings more sediment than the sea can

01:20.877 --> 01:23.928
carry away, the sediment builds out into a delta.

01:23.928 --> 01:29.126
That gives you five terms to know: drainage basin, channel, floodplain,

01:29.126 --> 01:30.682
estuary, and delta.

01:30.682 --> 01:34.031
Water in a river does not move at the same speed everywhere.

01:34.031 --> 01:37.045
On a bend, the fastest water swings to the outside.

01:37.045 --> 01:38.577
It erodes the bank there.

01:38.577 --> 01:42.563
On the inside of the bend the water is slow, so it drops sand

01:42.563 --> 01:46.253
and builds a bar. One side is cut away while the other side is

01:46.253 --> 01:50.201
built up, and over many years the whole bend moves sideways across

01:50.201 --> 01:53.219
the floodplain. This is the rule from the erosion video.

01:53.219 --> 01:56.203
Fast water erodes, and slow water deposits.

01:56.203 --> 01:59.938
If you can find where a river is fast and where it is slow, you

01:59.938 --> 02:03.953
can predict where land is being lost and where it is being gained.

02:03.953 --> 02:06.357
A floodplain is built by the river itself.

02:06.357 --> 02:09.549
When the river carries more water than its channel can hold, it

02:09.549 --> 02:10.739
spills over the banks.

02:10.739 --> 02:14.404
The moment the water leaves the channel, it slows down and drops

02:14.404 --> 02:17.835
its sediment. The coarsest sediment drops first, right beside

02:17.835 --> 02:21.077
the channel, and builds low ridges called natural levees.

02:21.077 --> 02:23.904
Finer mud spreads out across the rest of the plain.

02:23.904 --> 02:27.084
Flood after flood, this builds flat, fertile land.

02:27.084 --> 02:28.543
Here is the important point.

02:28.543 --> 02:30.404
The floodplain is part of the river.

02:30.404 --> 02:32.665
The river just does not use it every year.

02:32.665 --> 02:37.089
That is exactly what makes it look like safe, empty land to build

02:37.089 --> 02:40.346
on. A flood happens when the amount of water arriving, called

02:40.346 --> 02:43.269
the discharge, is more than the channel can carry.

02:43.269 --> 02:45.005
Two things make floods worse.

02:45.005 --> 02:47.125
The first is more water arriving.

02:47.125 --> 02:51.019
That means very heavy rain, ground that is already soaked, and

02:51.019 --> 02:54.906
city surfaces like concrete and asphalt, where rain cannot soak

02:54.906 --> 02:57.331
in and runs straight to the drains and the river.

02:57.331 --> 02:59.584
The second is less room for the water.

02:59.584 --> 03:01.324
Channels have been narrowed.

03:01.324 --> 03:03.244
Floodplains have been built on.

03:03.244 --> 03:06.913
Lakes and marshes that used to store water have been filled in.

03:06.913 --> 03:10.877
In December 2015, after weeks of heavy northeast monsoon rain,

03:10.877 --> 03:14.730
the Adyar and the Cooum overflowed, and large parts of Chennai

03:14.730 --> 03:17.165
were under water. Both causes were at work.

03:17.165 --> 03:18.943
Now apply this to Chennai.

03:18.943 --> 03:21.806
The Cooum and the Adyar are both short rivers.

03:21.806 --> 03:23.718
They do not start in mountains.

03:23.718 --> 03:27.476
They start on nearly flat land to the west of the city, so their

03:27.476 --> 03:29.737
slope is gentle and they flow slowly.

03:29.737 --> 03:33.445
Most of their water arrives in a few weeks of the northeast monsoon,

03:33.445 --> 03:34.852
from October to December.

03:34.852 --> 03:38.086
For the rest of the year there is very little natural flow, and

03:38.086 --> 03:40.332
much of what is in them is wastewater.

03:40.332 --> 03:43.793
At the coast, waves build sand bars across both river mouths,

03:43.793 --> 03:46.873
and in the dry season a bar can almost close a mouth.

03:46.873 --> 03:48.888
The Adyar ends in a broad estuary.

03:48.888 --> 03:52.007
The Cooum reaches the sea in a narrow mouth beside the harbor.

03:52.007 --> 03:55.144
People manage city rivers in two broad ways.

03:55.144 --> 03:56.890
The first is to channelize.

03:56.890 --> 04:00.250
You straighten the river and line it with walls, so water leaves

04:00.250 --> 04:03.369
the area quickly. The cost is that the flood moves downstream

04:03.369 --> 04:06.998
to someone else, and the storage that the floodplain used to provide

04:06.998 --> 04:09.217
is lost. The second is to restore.

04:09.217 --> 04:13.602
You give the river room, with wetlands, open banks, and a floodplain

04:13.602 --> 04:16.567
that still works. Water is stored and slowed down.

04:16.567 --> 04:20.206
The cost is land, and in a city that land is already being used

04:20.206 --> 04:22.606
by people. Neither choice is free.

04:22.606 --> 04:24.494
Each one moves the cost somewhere else.

04:24.494 --> 04:27.887
Keep that in mind when you read your two sources about the Cooum.

04:27.887 --> 04:29.901
Check yourself with three questions.

04:29.901 --> 04:31.448
Pause if you need to.

04:31.448 --> 04:35.250
One. Rain falls just on the far side of a river's divide.

04:35.250 --> 04:37.847
Does that water reach this river?

04:37.847 --> 04:39.936
Two. A river goes around a bend.

04:39.936 --> 04:42.946
Which bank erodes, the inside or the outside?

04:42.946 --> 04:46.875
Three. A river spills onto its floodplain and slows down.

04:46.875 --> 04:49.773
Which does it drop first, sand or fine mud?

04:49.773 --> 04:51.124
Here are the answers.

04:51.124 --> 04:54.691
No. Rain beyond the divide drains to a different river.

04:54.691 --> 04:58.185
The outside bank erodes, because the water is fastest there.

04:58.185 --> 05:01.714
And the sand drops first, right beside the channel, which is how

05:01.714 --> 05:03.181
natural levees are built.

05:03.181 --> 05:04.728
Now go on to today's task.
